= Choosing Our Enemies

In our media and campaign materials, who should we treat as our main opponent?

[? if enemies != 0 : Previously, we framed the [+ enemies +] as our chief opponent. Is a change of direction necessary? ?]

- @maga: Trumpism specifically - the authoritarian wing of the GOP.
- @gop_broad: The Republican Party as a whole.
- @establishment: The Democratic establishment itself, for failing to fight hard enough.
- @greens: The Green Party, for splitting the coalition.
- @corporate_power: Corporate power and big donors, regardless of party.
- @easy_discard

@maga
on-arrival: enemies = "Trumpist wing of the GOP"; progressive_strength += 5; establishment_dem_strength += 5; insurgent_dissent += 5; maga_share -= 0.3 if authoritarian_urgency >= 3;

Focusing our fire on Trumpism specifically is popular across most of the coalition, though the insurgent wing would rather be targeting corporate power and the establishment alike.

[? if authoritarian_urgency >= 3 : With authoritarian drift now a genuinely urgent concern, this framing lands with real force. ?]

@gop_broad
on-arrival: enemies = "Republican Party"; insurgent_strength += 5; insurgent_dissent -= 5; gop_estab_relation -= 5; maga_share += 0.2;

Treating the whole GOP as the enemy is popular with the base, but makes it harder to peel off establishment-wing Republicans - if anything, it may push them closer to Trump out of tribal loyalty.

@establishment
on-arrival: enemies = "Democratic establishment"; insurgent_strength += 8; insurgent_dissent -= 5; establishment_dem_dissent += 10; establishment_dem_strength -= 3; blue_dog_dissent += 5;

Aiming our fire inward, at the party's own establishment wing, energizes the insurgent base but deeply alarms the establishment and blue-dog factions, who see it as attacking the coalition from within.

@greens
on-arrival: enemies = "Green Party"; establishment_dem_strength += 5; green_relation -= 8; dirty_break_support -= 1;

Blaming the Greens for splitting the left vote is popular with the establishment and progressive wings, but the insurgent wing tends to see this as scapegoating rather than addressing why some voters are drifting left of the party in the first place.

@corporate_power
on-arrival: enemies = "corporate power and big donors"; insurgent_strength += 6; labor_left_strength += 5; insurgent_dissent -= 6; labor_left_dissent -= 6; donor_strike_progress += 1;

An anti-corporate message unites the insurgent and labor-left wings and plays well with the working-class base, but it makes the party's traditional donor network considerably more nervous.
